  [38;5;153musername[39m is logged in cleartext throughout, per your decision. [38;5;153mpassword[39m is never passed to the logger at all — and if
  someone later passes the whole form object, the deny-list catches it. Both layers, because the first one is discipline
  and the second one is code.

  Note what the [38;5;153mlogin()[39m rows imply: [38;5;153mlogin()[39m currently can't throw — it's a stub that returns a fixed object. The error
  row is for when it becomes a real [38;5;153mfetch[39m. I'd wrap the call in [38;5;153mtry/catch[39m now anyway, because the handler currently has
  no failure path at all, and a rejected login today would surface as an unhandled rejection with no log line.

  [1mGlobal handlers.[0m [38;5;153mwindow.addEventListener('error', ...)[39m and [38;5;153m'unhandledrejection'[39m both log at [38;5;153merror[39m with the message,
  source location, and stack when available. This is the part that catches what I didn't think to instrument, and it's
  why I'm comfortable recommending A over B.

  [1mOne thing I'd change while I'm in here.[0m [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m is declared and never used. The [38;5;153mlogin()[39m meta row above gives it a
  first real use, which is honest — but if you'd rather I leave dead code alone, say so and I'll drop [38;5;153mendpoint[39m from
  that record.

  Look right? Next section is error handling and testing, including a tooling question for you.

